\f1\fs22 Kathy did not like cells.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>She especially did not like cells dressed up as counselling rooms, the way institutions always did when they wanted you to think the violence was consensual. This one had a padded chair bolted to the floor, a table with rounded corners, and a small bowl of mints that looked like it had been installed by a committee in self-defence.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Outside the one-way glass, the High Elvis\'92s cabinet hovered in a half-circle, faces tight. The High Elvis stood at the centre, cape heavy, hands clasped behind his back like a man trying to remember what he\'92d been trained to do with them.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah sat with her hands folded over her belly, posture perfect, eyes too bright. Hayley leaned against the wall at the back of the viewing room, not quite blending into it, but giving it a go.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>All the Elvi in the viewing room wore earplugs except the High Elvis, who refused on principle, as if principles were the only thing in the Ghetto you couldn\'92t confiscate.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The Sages were in the room with Kathy, because of course they were. If the day was going to become weird, you wanted the weirdos closest to the action.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chilo stood near the table as if the table belonged to him. Solo wandered the perimeter, peering at bolts and corners with the clinical curiosity of someone shopping for weaknesses. Kleo sat in the padded chair opposite the patient, legs crossed, looking relaxed in a way that suggested he\'92d never once been trapped anywhere he didn\'92t want to be.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The patient was an Elvis in a robe the colour of stale tea. His sideburns were trimmed down to something modest and miserable. His hands were clasped in his lap with religious neatness.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A tag on the folder said: 
\fs20 CHANGE OF HABIT SERIES \'97 DR CARPENTER
\fs22 .\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dr Carpenter smiled at them with the serene certainty of a man who\'92d been given permission to stop thinking.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93My brothers and sisters,\'94 he said warmly.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy winced. The warmth was the wrong part.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chilo cleared his throat with the careful gentleness of someone about to poke a bear and then blame the bear for being rude.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Dr Carpenter,\'94 Chilo said, stressing the title like it was a test, \'93can you tell us what you heard?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I heard the Word,\'94 Dr Carpenter said. \'93It was placed into my heart through harmony.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy\'92s mind flashed, uninvited, to SARA readouts: patterns like language. Meaning travelling through air.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kleo leaned forward a fraction. \'93What was the Word?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dr Carpenter\'92s smile widened. \'93Praise be to\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Right,\'94 Kathy cut in. \'93We\'92re not doing the praise thing in here.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dr Carpenter regarded her with gentle pity, like she\'92d confessed to being left-handed, or an actual tax-payer.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You cannot ban truth,\'94 he said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley\'92s voice crackled through the intercom from the other room, flat and procedural. \'93No singing. No humming.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chilo reached into a small bag\'97where had he got a bag?\'97and produced a battered portable speaker.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy stiffened.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Rock and roll therapy,\'94 Chilo said. \'93A simple counter-frequency. We begin with the basics.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He pressed play.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A scratchy recording of \'93Grey Walls of Tyres\'94 (not one of Chanta\'92s best) rattled into the room at a volume that was meant to be harmless.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dr Carpenter listened, head tilted slightly.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>No hips. No footwork. No involuntary pelvis.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He closed his eyes and smiled like a saint.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Devil music,\'94 he murmured.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The High Elvis\'92s voice came through the intercom, sharper now. \'93It\'92s not working.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kleo tapped a fingernail lightly against his knee. \'93Of course it isn\'92t.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy shot him a look. \'93Do you want to expand on that, or just be smug about it?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kleo spread his hands in mild apology. \'93Belief isn\'92t a reflex. Belief is a structure. You don\'92t knock down a cathedral by playing a different song outside.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy didn\'92t like that he was right.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chilo shut the speaker off. Silence pressed in.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dr Carpenter sighed, content. \'93Better.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Okay,\'94 Kathy said. \'93Let\'92s try something that isn\'92t a nostalgia assault.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>She pulled out her tablet and opened a calm science documentary\'97finches, beaks, the slow violence of adaptation. She angled the screen so Dr Carpenter could see.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dr Carpenter leaned forward, polite interest arranged on his face like a mask.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Amazing,\'94 he said. \'93The Lord\'92s design.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy\'92s jaw tightened. \'93It\'92s evolution.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He nodded serenely. \'93The Lord\'92s evolution.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Solo made a small appreciative noise from the corner.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy ignored him with professional focus.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We\'92re going up a level,\'94 she said. \'93Language.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chilo stepped forward, voice formal, clean. \'93Latin,\'94 he said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dr Carpenter\'92s eyes widened slightly, as if pleased by the word itself.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chilo spoke carefully. \'93
\f2\i Ego sum filius Dei.
\f1\i0 \'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dr Carpenter\'92s chest rose and fell. His eyes grew wet. Then he repeated it with reverence, like he\'92d always known it. \'93
\f2\i Ego sum filius Dei
\f1\i0 ,\'94 he repeated.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy felt the hair on the back of her neck lift. \'93That,\'94 she murmured, \'93is not good.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The High Elvis\'92s voice snapped through the intercom, strained. \'93Stop feeding it.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chilo stopped. He looked faintly disappointed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy stared at Dr Carpenter and watched him sit, glowing with borrowed authority.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It\'92s copying,\'94 she said. \'93It\'92s\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It\'92s adopting,\'94 Solo corrected gently.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy bared her teeth in something approximating a smile. \'93It\'92s adopting like a parasite adopts your nutrients.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kleo\'92s smile never wavered. \'93If that metaphor comforts you.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Solo wandered closer, bright-eyed. \'93What about Sanskrit?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy blinked. \'93Sorry?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Solo nodded, pleased with himself. \'93Ancient. Sacred. Authoritative.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy looked at Kleo. Kleo didn\'92t tell him no.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Of course he didn\'92t.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chilo glanced toward the glass\'97toward the High Elvis, toward Sarah, toward the cabinet\'97then back at Dr Carpenter, weighing risk like a man weighing coins.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Very well,\'94 Chilo said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He spoke a line with deliberate cadence, temple-sounding. Old. Heavy. Serious. \'93
\f3 \uc0\u2341 \u2375 
\f1  
\f3 \uc0\u2360 \u2379 \u2344 
\f1  
\f3 \uc0\u2323 \u2398 \u2381 
\f1  
\f3 \uc0\u2327 \u2379 \u2342 
\f1  
\f3 \uc0\u2311 
\f1  
\f3 \uc0\u2309 \u2306 ,
\f1 \'94 he said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dr Carpenter\'92s face transformed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Not into confusion.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Into rapture.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He spoke back, suddenly, in Devanagari-shaped English, \'93
\f3 \uc0\u2344 \u2379 \u2341 \u2367 \u2344 \u2381 \u2327 
\f1  
\f3 \uc0\u2348 \u2369 \u2340 \u2381 
\f1  
\f3 \uc0\u2350 \u2344 
\f1  
\f3 \uc0\u2309 \u2352 \u2375 
\f1  
\f3 \uc0\u2351 \u2379 \u2313 
\f1 .\'94\'97syllables that sounded exotic in the mouth but collapsed into nonsense the moment Kathy\'92s brain tried to hold them.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy stared. \'93That is not Sanskrit,\'94 she said. \'93That\'92s English wearing Sanskrit as a costume.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dr Carpenter beamed at her as if she\'92d complimented his robe. He drew breath\'97\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley\'92s voice cracked through the intercom like a slap. \'93Do not let him sing.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Too late.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dr Carpenter began to hum.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Soft. Tender. The kind of melody you wanted to believe was harmless.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy felt it\'97immediate and humiliating. She hated it instantly. \'93Stop,\'94 she said, voice raw. \'93Stop that.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kleo rose, very calmly, and placed his hand\'97lightly\'97on Dr Carpenter\'92s shoulder.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The humming wavered.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kleo leaned in, voice low, and for the first time Kathy heard something in him that wasn\'92t humour or patience.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Listen to me,\'94 Kleo said. \'93You will stop now.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The humming faltered, then died.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dr Carpenter blinked, serene again, smiling as if forgiveness had just been administered.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy looked at Kleo, then at the other Sages, and felt the sick click of realisation. They weren\'92t trying to cure it. They were learning the operating system.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kleo straightened and looked at the High Elvis through the glass.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We can remove a belief,\'94 he said. \'93But not without replacing it.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>On the other side of the glass, the High Elvis\'92s shoulders sagged, just slightly.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah\'92s hand went to her belly.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy tasted fear, sharp as metal.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley\'92s hand rested near her belt, not touching anything, but ready.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy swallowed and forced the words out.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93So what do you replace it with?\'94 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kleo didn\'92t smile this time.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He looked at Dr Carpenter the way a surgeon looked at a tumour: without hatred, without affection, only with a cool interest in whether it could be cut out cleanly. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He glanced at Sarah through the glass\'97just a flicker of acknowledgement, as if naming her directly would make the room tilt.  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Praise the Dawkins,\'94 Kleo said, softly, like a test and a prayer at once. He rested his eyes with Dr Carpenter\'92s, and let him believe he had nothing to hide.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Dr Carpenter blinked.  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>For a heartbeat the serenity slipped. The borrowed smile faltered. His throat worked. His eyes sharpened as if, briefly, a person was trying to come back online.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Praise the Dawkins,\'94 Kleo said again, even softer than before. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Then, very quietly, as if the words had been pulled out of him by a hook:  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Praise the Dawkins,\'94 Dr Carpenter repeated. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy went cold. Not because he\'92d said it\'97people said all kinds of things under pressure\'97but because of what happened to his face afterward. The feverish glow didn\'92t return. The performance didn\'92t return.  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He sat there, breathing hard, looking suddenly\'85 anchored. Grounded. Like he\'92d remembered gravity.  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy\'92s mouth went dry. \'93That\'92s\'85\'94  \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kleo\'92s eyes stayed on Dr Carpenter. \'93Yes.\'94 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>On the other side of the glass, the High Elvis didn\'92t move. Sarah\'92s hand tightened over her belly. Hayley\'92s fingers flexed once, as if she\'92d felt the air shift. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy swallowed. The only thought she could manage was the worst one: 
\f2\i If you can swap a hymn out fo
